nplearn — portable EXG template matcher (the embedded half of exg-c)

No heap, no FILE, no threads. Floats in, scores out. Linux and Android
hosts link this; a Knight MCU builds only the two core files.

  nplearn.c       store, score, flash blob
  nplearn_filt.c  hp 2 Hz + notch + lp 40 Hz + pack
  nplearn_posix.c optional path save/load (host only)

MCU:

  cc -c nplearn/src/nplearn.c nplearn/src/nplearn_filt.c -Inplearn/include

  float wave[8][64], rms[8];
  uint8_t mask = 0;
  for (ch = 0; ch < 8; ch++) {
      if (npl_prep(wave[ch], &rms[ch], uv[ch], n, 125.f, 50.f) == 0)
          mask |= (uint8_t)(1u << ch);
  }
  npl_add(&L, "fist", wave, rms, mask);   /* record */
  npl_set_cube(&L, slot, cube512);        /* SoT: 8^3 bits */
  npl_set_smx(&L, slot, rows, nsec, fold);
  npl_score(&L, wave, rms, mask);
  npl_score_cube(&L, live_cube);          /* Jaccard on occupied cells */
  npl_score_smx(&L, live_rows, nsec);     /* L.best, L.score[] */

  unsigned char blob[npl_bound()];
  n = npl_export(&L, blob, sizeof blob);  /* write to flash */
  npl_import(&L, blob, n);
